+## §769 — a bot-wall notice that ate a search of our own code
+
+Opened by finishing §768's inventory (every URL the product shows resolves; the
+update manifest is served and signed, and `downloads.tokenade.net`'s bare-host
+404 is a missing index, not a link anyone is handed). The defect came from
+WATCHING THE TOOL WORK: a `grep -rn` for a vendor name during that inventory
+came back as bot-wall remediation with none of its matches.
+
+### The defect
+
+`stealth::detect_challenge` replaces what it fires on — deliberately, because an
+agent has to know it hit a wall rather than receive the wall as content. It is
+held off source code by `looks_like_source_or_doc`, which recognised a `grep`
+locator only when the path was absolute or opened with `./`.
+
+`grep -rn <pattern> <dir>` from a repository root prints a RELATIVE path.
+
+| | 1.1.9 | after |
+|---|---|---|
+| bytes | 657 → **559** | 657 → **657** |
+| matches delivered | **0 of 7** | 7 of 7 |
+| lines scoring a source signal | **0 of 7** | 7 of 7 |
+
+### What made it invisible
+
+A test named `detects_grep_output_with_absolute_paths` already carried the
+relative form on its FIRST line — and passed on the strength of its second,
+absolute line. One line of the fixture was doing all the work and the other
+proved nothing.
+
+> **A fixture line that cannot decide is not coverage.** The name promised the
+> relative case; only the absolute case was exercised.
+
+The same trap then caught the new test: M4 (drop the markup/space refusal)
+survived because in `<script src="https://a/b.js">:12:x` the FIRST colon is the
+one in `https:`, so the path field is `<script src="https` and the SEPARATOR
+clause refused it. Rewritten as `<a href=b/c>:12:x`, M4 dies. Third recurrence
+of §759's rule: the fixture must land where only the clause under test answers.
+
+6/6 mutations killed. Patchnote (1.1.9 reproduced).
+
+### The family, enumerated before moving on
+
+`source_signals.rs` names three detectors asking this question.
+`logs::looks_like_source_code` **deliberately** excludes `path:line` — wiring it
+in once made a four-shape build log decline — and the GraphQL SDL veto matches
+`crate::` mid-line. Neither shares the gap. All three consumers of the fixed
+predicate (`detect_challenge`, the JS check, `serp`) take the fix at once.
+
+### Instrument note
+
+Every attempt to PRINT the reproduction tripped the detector on my own terminal
+output, banner and all — including the `echo`s around it. Measurements have to
+be computed in-process and reported as numbers, never by displaying the blob.
+Same shape as §761.
+
+### Measured negative, same iteration — readers racing writers on the ledger
+
+Killed once by memory pressure for running alongside the full suite (the
+standing rule: never a heavy probe beside a suite). Re-run alone, bounded, every
+child under `ulimit -v 2 GB`: **974 reads against 4 concurrent writers, 0 non-zero
+exits, 0 malformed JSON** from `statusline` and `gain --json`. Do not reopen.
+
+## §770 — UTF-8 boundary truncation, from two competitors' same-day fixes
+
+Piste 1. Two independent repos shipped the class on 2026-09-06: pitlane-mcp
+`fix: make signature truncation UTF-8 safe` (a `truncate_chars` helper replacing
+`&sig[..90]`) and tokensave's `non-ASCII docs` among five reported bugs. Same
+shape as §762, where two same-day competitor fixes named a real class.
+
+### The sweep
+
+Byte-index slicing and `String::truncate` / `split_at` across production Rust,
+with `#[cfg(test)]` modules excluded by brace-matching (the classifier
+over-includes where the attribute is indented, so every hit was read).
+
+| site | verdict |
+|---|---|
+| `gitlog.rs` subject / author | **already safe** (`char_indices().nth`, `find`) |
+| `ansible.rs` failure lines | **already safe**, comment names Unicode hostnames |
+| `semantic.rs`, `disclose/scoped.rs` | **already safe** (`is_char_boundary` walk-back) |
+| `agent_sqlite_usage.rs` | **already safe**, guards all three offsets |
+| `yaml.rs`, `filter.rs`, `web_fetch.rs` `split_at` | safe: indices come from `find` |
+| `rag.rs`, `cargo/build.rs` | safe: slices of `&[String]` / `Vec<&str>` |
+| **`site_readers.rs` × 2** | **PANICS** |
+| **`agent/tools.rs::clamp`** | **PANICS** |
+
+36 `is_char_boundary` uses in production: this is established discipline here,
+and the sweep's real finding is how few sites were missed.
+
+### The two that were missed
+
+`crates_api` cuts `updated_at` and each version's `created_at` to ten
+characters with `&d[..d.len().min(10)]`. Measured:
+
+> `end byte index 10 is not a char boundary; it is inside 'é' (bytes 9..11)`
+
+`clamp` is the more interesting one, because it MEANT to be safe: the comment
+reads "land on a char boundary" and the boundary walk is right there — but the
+slice that produces the value being walked, `&s[..max.min(s.len())]`, panics
+first. **The guard sat one line below the thing it was guarding.**
+
+### No patchnote, deliberately
+
+Neither is reachable with hostile input in a shipped default build.
+`crates_api` is dispatched by host, so its body comes from crates.io, whose
+timestamps are ASCII ISO-8601; `mod agent` is behind the non-default
+`light-agent` feature, shelved 2026-07 as benched net-negative. Recorded as
+HARDENING. Writing a patchnote for a panic no user can reach would be the
+§744 trap — dressing up a score until it stops meaning anything.
+
+Both routed to the helper that already existed, `caps::truncate_at_char_boundary`
+— see [[feedback_route_to_the_ceiling_that_exists]] — rather than a new one.
+6/6 mutations killed, including "cut to nothing" and an off-by-one, so the arms
+answer for the cut as well as for the boundary.
+
+## §771 — a symlinked directory answered questions about the project
+
+Piste 1, from diffctx `fix: symlink containment, --full parity, tau as Option,
+NaN, exit 124` (2026-09-06). Their case: `git ls-files` reports the LINK, while
+`is_file()` and `metadata()` follow it, so `repo/evil -> /etc/shadow` entered
+the changed-file universe. Asked the same question here.
+
+### The defect
+
+`Index::build` and `Index::rebuild_incremental` both branch on
+`path.is_dir()`, which FOLLOWS a symlink, and descend. git records a symlinked
+directory as mode 120000 and never descends it; tokenade already follows
+`.gitignore`, so its project model was git's everywhere except here.
+
+Cold-path, one arm per fresh directory (the index lives in
+`~/.tokenade/index.db` keyed by path AND version, so deleting `.tokenade`
+clears nothing — three contaminated readings before this was noticed):
+
+| | no link | with one link |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1.1.9 | 24 ms, 97 B | **610 ms, 6 087 B, 1 490 files** |
+| fixed | 128 ms, 97 B | 129 ms, 97 B |
+
+The fixed arm is a debug build, so it is compared only against ITSELF — §759's
+rule. The build-independent number is the output: 6 087 B naming files
+`git ls-files` does not list, against 97 B.
+
+### Both walkers, and the mutations that prove it
+
+Two copies of the same loop, which is exactly how this repo's most frequent
+defect happens — a fix landing on one consumer and missing the twin (≈12×).
+So M2 (drop the guard from `build` only) and M3 (drop it from
+`rebuild_incremental` only) are separate mutations, and both die. 6/6.
+
+The test's own arms: a real subdirectory still walked (kills "skip every
+directory"), and a symlinked FILE still indexed — that costs one file, not a
+tree, and pinning it fixes the scope of the change.
+
+The loop guard STAYS. It is not made redundant by this — see §763, where I
+deleted a clause because one consumer could not reach it and was wrong.
+
+### Also swept, clean
+
+Every other recursive walker: `feature_cli/packing.rs` uses `file_type()`
+(lstat) and is bounded by `MAX_PACK_FILES`; `agent_jsonl_usage.rs` is
+depth-bounded; the `code_intel.rs` and `markdown.rs` walks are inside
+`#[cfg(test)]` source sweeps. `project_scaffold::dir_size_mb` recurses
+unbounded through `is_dir()` but only over the fastembed model cache — noted,
+not fixed, and its doc-comment claiming it "walks one level deep" is wrong.
+
+Symlink LOOPS were probed live before any of this and terminate: `map` 211 ms,
+`query` 32 ms on a tree with `vendor/sub/back -> vendor` and `self_link -> .`.
+
+## §772 — an accent killed a whole listing
+
+Piste 1, from tokensave 22951bcf `fix(docs): match the sidecar suffix on bytes,
+not a char split`: `split_at(len - SUFFIX.len())` panicked on
+`docs/Ativação bbbb.md`, aborting `init`, `sync --force` and `doctor`.
+
+This is §770's class arriving through a shape §770's sweep could NOT see. That
+sweep matched `&x[..N]` and `.truncate(...)`; the failing shape is TAIL
+arithmetic — `&s[s.len() - N..]`, `split_at(s.len() - N)`. Swept separately, 27
+sites, and the triage is one rule:
+
+> **`ends_with` PROVES the boundary.** `token[..token.len() - suf.len()]` after
+> `token.ends_with(suf)` is sound, because a matching suffix cannot start mid
+> character. `semantic.rs`, `web.rs` × 2 and `ls_recursive.rs` are all guarded
+> that way; `disk.rs` matches the last BYTE against ASCII; `markdown.rs`
+> subtracts a `trim_start()` length, which is whitespace.
+
+One site subtracts a CONSTANT instead, and nothing proves that offset:
+
+```rust
+let p_short = if p.len() > 46 { format!("…{}", &p[p.len() - 45..]) } else { p };
+```
+
+Reproduced end to end on 1.1.9 by pointing `TOKENADE_SEMANTIC_DB` at a scratch
+db and inserting one row:
+
+> `start byte index 13 is not a char boundary; it is inside 'é' (bytes 12..14)`
+
+The header prints, then the process dies. Every row is lost and the message
+names a byte offset, not a project.
+
+### The fix, and why it is EXTRACTED
+
+Counting characters, not bytes — which also matches the `{:<46}` column better
+than bytes ever did. Pulled out of `cmd_semantic_status` into
+`shorten_path_for_column`, because a decision made inside a command function is
+one no test can reach (§763, third time).
+
+7 mutations, 6 run and killed (one was a no-op and the harness SKIPPED it rather
+than reporting a false survivor). The arms that carry them: exact character
+count kills both off-by-ones; a 40-character accented path that is 80 BYTES
+kills "measure bytes"; degenerate widths 0 and 1 kill the two underflow guards;
+and an ASCII arm asserts the new code returns byte-for-byte what the old slice
+returned where the old slice was correct.
+
+## §773 — the two walkers measured different bytes
+
+Piste 1, from pitlane-mcp `fix: apply the same exclusion policy to watcher
+updates and initial indexing` (2026-09-06) — this repo's most recurrent class,
+and §771 had just shown tokenade carries two copies of that loop. So the two
+were DIFFED rather than assumed equal.
+
+### What the diff found
+
+Two differences, and only one of them matters:
+
+1. **`synth_ext` — dead.** `build` maps nine extensionless names (`Makefile`,
+   `Dockerfile`, `Rakefile`, `Gemfile`, `Procfile`, `Caddyfile`, `Justfile`,
+   `Vagrantfile`, `Brewfile`) to synthetic extensions and admits them;
+   `rebuild_incremental` has no such table. It costs nothing, because there is
+   **no `LangSpec` and no tree-sitter grammar for any of the nine**, so
+   `extract_file` discards every one. Confirmed by probe: build's `mtimes` held
+   only `a.rs`. Left in place, pinned by the new test, so that giving them a
+   parser has to touch both walkers.
+
+2. **The size cap measured different bytes.** `build` used
+   `DirEntry::metadata`, which on Unix does NOT traverse a symlink, so a link
+   to an oversized file measured as the link — a few bytes — and slipped past
+   the 1 MiB cap. `rebuild_incremental` used `fs::metadata`, which traverses,
+   and applied it.
+
+### The defect, end to end on 1.1.9
+
+```
+fresh build      -> fn huge_symbol — linked.rs:1
+after one edit   -> no symbol matching 'huge_symbol'
+```
+
+One command, one tree, two answers, and a later full rebuild brings it back.
+`build` was the wrong side: its own comment says the cap is there to skip
+"minified bundles, generated artefacts, vendored blobs", and the bytes that get
+read are the target's.
+
+### The invariant, not just the fix
+
+The guard is `both_walkers_select_the_same_files`: build, edit one file,
+rebuild incrementally, assert the selected file sets are EQUAL — plus what that
+set must be, so "select nothing" and "select everything" both fail. 6/6
+mutations, including M4, the MIRROR defect (switch the incremental walker to
+lstat instead), and both `>`/`>=` boundary flips, which the fixture earns by
+carrying a file of exactly `MAX_INDEX_FILE_BYTES`.
+
+## §774 — a telemetry check that cost the square of the page
+
+Piste 3. Found by a 40-input hostile-input fuzz over `web` and `read` (deep
+nesting, unbalanced tags, unterminated attributes and raw-text elements, NUL,
+invalid UTF-8, lone surrogates, huge single lines, ragged CSV, ANSI soup,
+entity floods…). One hit: `read` on 1 MB of `&amp;`.
+
+### Attribution — four hypotheses, three of them wrong
+
+Every stage was TIMED, not read. Three plausible culprits died:
+
+| stage | verdict |
+|---|---|
+| `decode_entities` (90 sequential `String::replace`) | linear, 4/8/16/33 ms |
+| `markdown::outline` | 0 ms |
+| `web::html_to_markdown` | linear, 35/66/135/264 ms |
+| `auto::compact_with_path_hint` (whole) | linear, 38/67/135/275 ms |
+| `shrink_only_post_passes` | 0–1 ms |
+| **`filter::structured_or_generic`** | **80/312/1262/5066 ms** |
+| **`structured_still_speaks_for`** | **83/324/1296/5160 ms** |
+
+The 90-replace loop was the obvious suspect and looked damning. It is linear,
+and the NUMERIC entity — which decodes to the same character — is slower than
+the named one. That asymmetry (`&amp;` 1452 ms vs `&#38;` 127 ms end to end)
+was the real clue: it pointed at work being REDONE, not work being slow.
+
+### The defect
+
+`structured_still_speaks_for` caps the LINES it samples
+(`SPEAKS_FOR_SAMPLE_LINES = 500`) and does not cap the WORDS inside the winning
+line. Entity-heavy HTML is one long line, so `words` held ~512 000 copies of
+`"amp"`, each searched for in a haystack that also grows with the input and
+never matches. Input size × output size.
+
+Its only consumer is `debug_log::log` — the comment above the call site says so
+outright, "Deliberately telemetry and NOT a widened ceiling." 85 seconds of a
+10 MB page bought one optional log line.
+
+### Fix and numbers
+
+`.take(SPEAKS_FOR_MAX_WORDS)` at 64, in the HELPER, so all three production
+callers get it. Same debug binary, same inputs: 2782 ms → 340 ms at 160 KB, and
+600/1138/2193/4391 up to 2.56 MB — 2x a doubling. Output byte-identical.
+
+6/6 mutations, and the arms are two-sided: a cap that is too LARGE fails
+(64 words present then 136 absent — share collapses to a third), and a cap that
+is too SMALL fails (six of ten words kept, the exact minimum share, where the
+first word is one of the missing four). Plus a deadlined arm for the quadratic
+itself, in the §736 style.
+
+### Instrument note
+
+The before/after output comparison first reported DIFFERS. It was the harness:
+`diff <(new) <(old)` runs the new binary FIRST, which registers the content in
+the dedup store, so the second run sees a duplicate and says so. Separate runs
+into files: identical. Fourth instrument trap on this axis.
+
+## §775 — two defects in the rule that spares a test-runner status line
+
+Piste 3, continuing §774's fuzz onto the entry points it had not covered.
+Corpus rebuilt around SCALING shapes (one long line of entities / words / JSON,
+many identical lines, two interleaved shapes, deep indent, ANSI redraw, wide
+CSV, stack frames, diff-like, base64-ish, paths, key=value) and run through
+`read` AND `wrap`, flagging ms-per-MB as well as crashes.
+
+One hit, on both commands: `key=value token=abc123 id=42 ` repeated to 2 MB on
+ONE line. `read` and `wrap` alike, so the cost was in what they SHARE.
+
+### Narrowing (every step timed, none read)
+
+`compact` linear · `structured_or_generic` 0 ms (kind is Unknown) ·
+post-passes linear ⇒ 473 ms of an 11 998 ms command. The rest was
+`disclose::maybe_stash_bytes`, and inside it `redact::redact`, and inside THAT
+— using tokenade's own `TOKENADE_PROFILE=1` rather than more hand probes —
+`rd:keyword`: **329 ms on 507 KB, 4 901 ms on 2.03 MB.**
+
+An instrument check first: `ensure_stashed_bytes` calls `opportunistic_evict`,
+which walks the stash dir, and my loop ADDS a file per iteration — so bigger
+inputs also met a bigger directory. Ran the sizes in REVERSE: same curve. Not
+the instrument.
+
+### Defect 1 — a panic
+
+```rust
+["PASS","FAIL","SKIP","BENCH"].iter()
+    .any(|k| rest.len() >= k.len() && rest[..k.len()].eq_ignore_ascii_case(k))
+```
+
+`rest[..4]` on a `&str` lands inside a multibyte character. Reproduced on 1.1.9
+through BOTH `read` and `wrap`: a 160 KB log with one `--- aéé pass=…` line dies
+with `end byte index 4 is not a char boundary; it is inside 'é'`. Compared as
+BYTES now — which is what ASCII keywords always meant.
+
+Third sighting of the §770 class, and the first one that is REACHABLE by
+ordinary input: a YAML document separator opens with those same dashes.
+
+### Defect 2 — the quadratic
+
+The veto answered "is this match on a status line?" per MATCH, by
+`hay[..at].rfind('\n')` and then `format!` of everything between the line start
+and the match. On one long line that is a full-input scan AND a full-input
+allocation, per match. Now `status_line_ranges` computes them once per text and
+the per-match answer is a binary search — usually against an empty vector.
+
+Debug, same binary: 1014/2156/4812/12230 ms → **937/1840/3629/7223**, ratios
+1.96/1.97/1.99. Exactly linear.
+
+### Mutations: 7/7, after two fixture repairs
+
+M5 (`split` for `split_inclusive`) and M6 (`at > e`) SURVIVED the first pass —
+both fixture gaps. M5 drifts offsets by one PER LINE, so a three-line fixture
+still overlaps; it needs a status line 50 lines in. M6 differs only for a match
+starting exactly at a line boundary; it needs an assignment as the first bytes
+of the line AFTER a status line. And M2 (recompute the ranges per match) needed
+the timing fixture TRIPLED, because one memchr per match still fits under a
+5 s deadline at 1 MB.
+
+## §776 — a fixed byte jump over an assumed-ASCII timestamp
+
+Piste 3. §770, §772 and §775 each found the byte-slice class by enumerating
+CODE SHAPES, and each sweep missed the shape the next one found. So this
+iteration attacked it from the other side: **inject a multibyte character at
+every offset of realistic line shapes and see what dies**, which needs no
+theory about how the code is written.
+
+20 shapes (log, YAML, JSON, key=value, PEM, ssh key, markdown table, git
+commit, diff hunk, python frame, URL, HTML, JS stack, kubectl header…) × 4
+characters (`é`, `界`, `🚀`, NBSP) × every offset = 2 568 variants, padded past
+the stash threshold and run through `read` and `wrap`. Batched, with a bisect
+so a panicking batch names the exact variant.
+
+**Harness validated against 1.1.9 first** — a fuzz that reports nothing is
+worth nothing unless it can find a known bug. It found a NEW one instead:
+
+```
+2026-09-07 12:00:0é0 INFO service=api latency=12ms
+-> logs.rs:597  end byte index 19 is not a char boundary; it is inside 'é'
+```
+
+6 panicking variants on 1.1.9, all in the timestamp. `read` only; `wrap` takes
+a route that does not reach it.
+
+### The defect, and the sibling that was right
+
+```rust
+if i + 19 <= bytes.len() && bytes[i].is_ascii_digit()
+    && bytes[i+4] == b'-' && bytes[i+7] == b'-'
+    && (bytes[i+10] == b'T' || bytes[i+10] == b' ')
+    && bytes[i+13] == b':' && bytes[i+16] == b':'
+{ let start = i; i += 19; … let val = &line[start..i]; }
+```
+
+Five separators and ONE digit checked; the other thirteen digit positions never
+were. `i += 19` is nineteen CHARACTERS only if the window is all ASCII.
+
+The contrast is in the same file: `is_uuid_at` validates **all 36** positions,
+so its `i += 36` is sound by construction. `ghactions::has_iso_timestamp` is
+also safe — it requires byte 19 to be `Z` or `.`, so every later index is a
+boundary. One incomplete guard among three.
+
+Fixed as `is_iso_timestamp_at`, in `is_uuid_at`'s idiom. Fuzz on the fixed
+binary: **0 of 2 568**.
+
+### 7/7, after one fixture repair
+
+M7 (stop checking the separators) survived the first pass: every fixture had
+correct digits AND correct separators, so nothing could tell the two apart.
+Five strings, each wrong in exactly ONE separator position, kill it — the same
+shape as §775's M5/M6 and §759's rule, for the third time this session.
+
+## §777 — two axes swept, both clean, and a harness lesson
+
+Piste 3, same empirical method as §776 with different mutation operators.
+
+### Truncation — NEGATIVE, do not reopen
+
+12 realistic documents (html, json, yaml, pem, ssh key, log, ansi, markdown,
+diff, csv, key=value, uuid) truncated at **every byte offset**, byte-level so a
+cut can land inside a multibyte character, padded past the stash threshold and
+run through `read` and `wrap`. **939 truncations, 0 panics, 0 timeouts** on the
+fixed tree.
+
+> **The harness lied first, and the canary caught it.** A doc carrying §776's
+> known-bad line was included precisely so a silent run could be disbelieved.
+> The first pass reported 0 hits on 1.1.9 — where that line certainly panics —
+> because every probe file was written as `probe.dat`, and **routing is by
+> EXTENSION**: §774 already measured `.html` at 1455 ms against `.txt` at
+> 104 ms for the same bytes. With real extensions the canary fired 34 times.
+> A fuzz that cannot reach the code proves nothing about it.
+
+### Scaling — NEGATIVE across 19 shapes
+
+Each shape built at N and 2N, ratio reported (≈2 linear, ≈4 quadratic): words
+on one line, identical lines, unique lines, two interleaved shapes, timestamps,
+uuids, flat json, json array, yaml, csv, markdown table, html rows, ansi
+redraw, paths, stack frames, diff, base64, secrets — **all 1.83 to 2.12**.
+
+The only two quadratics in the whole sweep were the ones already fixed this
+session, and the probe re-found them independently on 1.1.9: `entities_html`
+3.59 (§774) and `kv_one_line` 3.91 (§775). On the fixed tree the same two
+measure **1.89 and 2.00** — an independent confirmation of both fixes, from a
+probe written after them and not shaped around them.
+
+### And a defect, from piste 1 — the worktree the cache could not see
+
+tokf `feat(rewrite): leave git unrewritten inside linked worktrees` named the
+shape: in a LINKED WORKTREE `.git` is a FILE, not a directory. Asked the same
+question here.
+
+`hooks/replay.rs::state_fingerprint` decides whether a repeated read-only
+command can be answered without re-running it. For a `git ` command it
+fingerprints `<cwd>/.git/HEAD` (branch moves), `<cwd>/.git/index` (staging) and
+the cwd mtime. In a worktree neither path exists, and the helper hashes an
+absent file as a CONSTANT — so the whole git half was fixed and only the
+directory mtime remained. Replay is on by default (`TOKENADE_NO_REPLAY=1`
+disables it), so `git status` could be served stale after a `git add` or a
+branch switch for the length of the freshness window.
+
+Measured on the fixture: `.git` reads `gitdir: …/.git/worktrees/linked`, that
+directory holds `HEAD` (`ref: refs/heads/feat`, a DIFFERENT branch from the
+main checkout) and `index`, and `linked/.git/HEAD` does not exist.
+
+Fixed by `git_dir_of`, best-effort: anything unreadable or unparseable falls
+back to `<cwd>/.git`, so the cache still fails toward re-running.
+
+**6/7, and the seventh is unkillable BY CONSTRUCTION and verified as such.**
+Three of the first four survivors were fixture gaps — the pointer in the
+fixture was ABSOLUTE so the relative branch never ran, and the HEAD/index arms
+rode on the cwd mtime, which a `git add` moves anyway. Rewriting HEAD and the
+index in place (same bytes, new mtime, nothing else touched) isolates them.
+M3 (drop the `is_dir` early return) cannot die: `read_to_string` on a directory
+already returns Err — probed, `is a directory` — so the line is intent, not a
+gate. Recorded in the code as such rather than dressed up, and distinct from
+the §744 case where a survivor meant the SUITE was blind.
+
+### Two of the repo's own guards caught the §777 test, and one working-tree note
+
+The worktree test first shelled out to `git`, and two existing guards refused
+it in turn: `every_spawn_either_hides_its_console_or_is_listed_as_wanting_one`
+(a spawn must call `configure_no_window` or be declared) and
+`every_process_launcher_either_guards_its_work_or_says_why_not` ("who kills
+this when tokenade is SIGKILLed?"). Both were right, and the second pointed at
+a better test: the fingerprint reads exactly three paths, so the worktree
+layout is now built BY HAND — no spawn, hermetic, and it no longer asserts
+nothing on a machine without git, which the shelling version silently did.
+
+Separately, a suite run left the repo's own agent-rules files stripped of their
+scaffold block (9 files, **278 deletions, 0 insertions**). NOT a test bug and
+not a defect: `project_scaffold::sync_gate_state` removes every tokenade prompt
+from a project's rules files while the licence gate is active and restores them
+after, recording what it stripped in `.tokenade/scaffold.retracted`. Restored
+from HEAD — verified safe first, since zero insertions meant no other
+uncommitted work lived in those files. The marker is left as it is: the restore
+path updates an existing block in place rather than appending, so a stale
+marker refreshes rather than duplicates.
