+## §782 — content wearing our own markers (CLOSED)
+
+Piste 3, the axis named in the prompt: **input carrying OUR OWN markers**. Two
+weight accumulators read a count back out of rendered text — `filter::run_weight`
+(`(×N)`) and `disclose::original_weight` (`(×N)` and `… [N lines elided by
+tokenade] …`) — and neither can tell a marker it wrote from a line of content
+shaped like one. A saved transcript, a CI log of a tokenade run, a pasted banner
+all carry them.
+
+**Measured before/after**
+
+| probe | before | after |
+|---|---|---|
+| 2 000-line log, one `(×900000)` content line, 1 825 folded | footer: `standing for 901824 line(s)` | no claim |
+| 9 000-line file, one `… [900000 lines elided…] …` line | `(elided 8970 lines, standing for 908969 line(s))` | `(elided 8970 lines)` |
+| same with a count near `usize::MAX` | **`attempt to add with overflow`**, output lost (debug; release wrapped silently) | summary delivered |
+
+**Fix — a provenance ceiling.** `filter::note_folded(n)` records the TRUE count
+at every site that collapses lines (`template_fold` ×2, `mcp_compact`, `dmesg`,
+`lsof`, `render_kept`'s gaps, `stacktrace`'s elision); every claim is capped by
+`elided + folded_ceiling()`, and all the sums saturate. A process that folded
+nothing can make no claim, which is exactly what forgery reaches.
+
+**Patchnote: filter side only.** `render_kept`'s footer shipped in 1.1.9
+(confirmed at `4d40f95b:filter.rs:3028,3066`). `disclose::original_weight` did
+NOT — it came in with the 1.1.10 hardening, so that half is a defect introduced
+and fixed inside one unpublished version and gets no entry.
+
+**Mutations 9/9**, after four repairs — three of them instrument or fixture
+faults, not code gaps:
+
+- The harness reported **BUILD-FAIL on all seven arms** because its build check
+  omitted `--features srcsweep`. A verdict every arm shares is a fact about the
+  harness.
+- **M9's filter matched no test**, and `run()` reads "0 passed" as failure, so a
+  mutation nothing covered reported KILLED. Re-run by hand against
+  `e2e_a_two_stage_fold_states_the_original_magnitude`: genuinely killed.
+- **M2 survived** a fixture asserting `!footer.contains("900000")` — the claim is
+  a SUM, so the forged 900000 renders as 901824 and a substring test never
+  matches. *Assert the invariant, not the forged digits.*
+- The real e2e two-stage guard then caught a REAL gap: `render_kept`'s own
+  elision markers carry a true count I had not recorded, so a legitimate
+  second-stage claim was being suppressed. That test is the anti-vacuity control
+  and it earned its keep.
+
+**And the cap was self-feeding at first**: `render_kept` noted its own gaps and
+then read the ceiling AFTER, so the call vouched for itself and a 2 000-line
+input still announced 3 650. The ceiling is snapshotted BEFORE the call now —
+the markers in the input belong to EARLIER passes, and this call's own gaps are
+`elided_total`, already counted separately. Mutation M8 holds it.
+
+## §783 — the hostile-input axis swept clean (5 NEGATIVES, no defect)
+
+Continuation of §782 across the rest of piste 3. **No code change, no patchnote.**
+Recorded so none of it is re-mined.
+
+**1. A content-borne marker does not suppress folding.** Hypothesis: a file whose
+first line carries `expand-ref <12-hex>`, `[tokenade: …]` or `§ref:<hash>§` reads
+as already folded and is delivered whole. MEASURED on a 298 KB log through both
+`read` and `wrap`, four variants: **99% saved in all eight runs**. `already_folded`
+gates booking and nudges, not the fold — the comments at `hookwrap.rs:92-97` say so
+and they are right.
+
+**2. An endless source is already bounded.** `wrap` on `yes` and on `cat
+/dev/urandom`: **RSS flat at 71–73 MB over 24 s**, twelve samples each, no growth.
+`drain_capped_keeping_tail(so, DEFAULT_STDOUT_CAP)` caps it AND returns a
+truncation flag that reaches `truncation_banner` — capped and honest, the §745
+lesson already applied. It never terminates, which is correct: neither does the
+native command.
+
+**3. `agent/tools.rs::fold_pipe`'s `wait_with_output` is not a hole.** It looks
+like the uncapped slurp its sibling `wrap` avoids, but the child is always
+tokenade itself (`web -`, `execute --script -`), which caps its own stdout, and
+the result is clamped to 24 000 chars. The write-then-read shape cannot deadlock
+either: both children read stdin to EOF before emitting.
+
+**4. Malformed declared formats are linear.** 12 shapes × 2 sizes through `read`
+— PEM that never closes, a BEGIN flood, nested BEGINs, a PGP header flood, one
+40× oversized ssh key body, an unknown key type, near-miss uuid/MAC/timestamp,
+forged `(×10^15)` runs, unterminated quotes, unclosed JSON: **every ratio between
+0.59 and 2.04**, nothing above the 2.6 flag.
+
+**5. §782 introduced no false suppression.** Enumerated every production emitter
+whose marker `run_weight` / `original_weight` can read — `template_fold` ×2,
+`mcp_compact`, `dmesg`, `lsof`, `render_kept`'s gaps, `stacktrace`'s elision:
+**7 of 7 record their fold**. A legitimate claim cannot be capped away.
+
+**Instrument faults, both mine, both caught before they became findings:**
+- `grep_count.rs`'s `total_matches += n` reads a u64 out of text and looked like
+  §782's twin. It is **off the live route**: `grep -c` goes
+  `grep_dispatcher → filter::grep_count → grep_count_fold` (a declarative spec).
+  The §755 trap, second time — *a compactor file is not evidence of a route*.
+- A scan for source files no `mod` declares reported **916 orphans including
+  `dmesg.rs`**, which I had edited that hour and which compiled. `build.rs`
+  generates the mod lists into `OUT_DIR`. *A structural scan that contradicts a
+  fact you just established is wrong about the structure.*
+
+## §784 — the ambiguity caveat counted its own display window (CLOSED)
+
+Piste 1: `git fetch` over the 281 tracked repos, **18 moved**. Two questions
+transferred; one paid.
+
+- `caveman c63362d` mentions `ghs_` redaction. Checked ours: **all six GitHub
+  prefixes present** (`ghp_ gho_ ghu_ ghs_ ghr_ github_pat_`, `redact/rules.rs:251-256`).
+  Negative.
+- **`sem d1ed948`** — *"return candidates on stdout for ambiguous names, never
+  empty"*. Ours already never returns empty: a miss answers `no symbol matching
+  'try_compat' — closest indexed: env_compat, COMPAT, try_compact`. But asking
+  their question of our tool found the defect one line over.
+
+**The defect.** `cmd_query` computed the ambiguity caveat as
+`hits.iter().filter(|s| s.name == q).count()` — over `hits`, which `find_in`
+caps at 30. So the caveat could never name more than 30 declarations however
+many existed, and the same answer contradicted itself:
+
+```
+[30 of 972 matches shown — narrow the name to see the rest]
+… [by NAME — 30 declarations share it, the call graph cannot tell them apart]
+```
+
+on a name **927** indexed symbols declare. The caveat is what tells the agent how
+far to trust the callers line, so understating it by 31× is the §708/§782 class
+again: a number true of its own stage, presented as the answer.
+
+**Fix.** `Index::count_exact_declarations(name, scope)` counts over the index,
+scoped exactly as `find_in` searches; `declarations_for` is the seam so the
+mistake is expressible and killable. **30 → 927.** Shipped in 1.1.9
+(`4d40f95b:code_intel.rs:340`), so it carries a patchnote.
+
+**Why the caveat can never be wrongly SUPPRESSED**, checked rather than assumed:
+`match_rank` sorts name-exact above substring, so with ≥2 exact declarations both
+are inside the 30-row window and `exact > 1` holds. The damage was confined to
+the number.
+
+**Mutations 5/5.** M5 (`declarations > 1` → `> 0`) survived my two new tests and
+is killed by two pre-existing guards — my harness filter was too narrow, not the
+suite. Verified by running the whole `code_intel` module against the mutation
+rather than assuming coverage.
+
+## §785 — a query in the wrong case lost half its answer (CLOSED)
+
+Started as a SWEEP of the class §784 made three of (§708, §782, §784): a count
+computed over a capped view, printed as the answer. The sweep came back clean —
+`impact` (`fn (129): … +117 more`), `map` (`[80 of 108 directories shown]`,
+`top files (10 of 1611)`), `render_kind_line` (which documents the raw-vs-distinct
+trap it resolves), the diff/gitlog/json banners (`370 more names elided` on 400
+input files). §784 was the only instance.
+
+**The defect the sweep found instead** came from checking an assumption I had
+made twice from reading rather than running. `Index::match_rank` compares
+`s.name.to_lowercase()`, so `find_in` matches without regard to case. Every step
+after it compares exactly — `count_exact_declarations`, `sole_declaration_file`,
+and `callers_with_total`'s `c.callee_name == name`. So a query in the wrong case
+listed the declarations and then said nothing about callers:
+
+```
+query Run → called by: Caller (main.go:6), Other (main.go:10) [6 declarations share it]
+query run → the same six declarations, and NO callers line
+```
+
+One index, one symbol, two answers — on a command whose help promises "where is
+this defined, and who calls it", and whose `graph_available` branch exists
+precisely to stop silence reading as "nothing calls it". Shipped in 1.1.9
+(`4d40f95b:code_intel.rs:341` passes the raw `q`); patchnote written.
+
+**Fix.** `resolved_spelling` resolves to what the index holds, and only when a
+SINGLE spelling answers — a tree holding both `Run` and `run` keeps them apart,
+because those are different symbols. `attribution_for` then returns the name, the
+count and the sole-declaration file as ONE unit, because the three have to agree:
+a count taken for one spelling beside a file taken for another describes two
+different symbols.
+
+**Mutations 6/6**, after one repair. M5 (pin attribution under the TYPED name)
+survived because the decision lived inline in `cmd_query`, which prints and so
+cannot be unit-tested — the extraction into `attribution_for` is what made the
+mistake expressible, and it earned two more arms (M5, M6). Widening the harness
+filter from four test names to the whole `code_intel::query_modal` module was
+part of the same repair; §784's M5 had already shown that filter was too narrow.
+
+**Control:** `count_exact_declarations` adds a full symbol scan per name. Timed
+on this tree (25 838 symbols): single query 983 ms after vs 970 before, batch of
+eight 1382 vs 1385 — noise.
+
+## §786 — the sibling that kept the typed name (CLOSED)
+
+Direct continuation of §785, taking this codebase's most frequent defect shape
+seriously: *proven on one consumer, not the sibling* (~12 occurrences). §785
+fixed the TEXT path; the sweep asked who else looks a name up. Two production
+sites share the `callers_of_declaration` / `callers_with_total` pair —
+`callers_line` (fixed) and **`callers_json`, which still received the raw `q`**.
+
+```
+query Run  --json → count 6, callers_total 2
+query run  --json → count 6, callers [] , callers_total 0
+```
+
+Worse than the text half: `callers_json`'s own doc says `null`, not `[]`, is for
+"we could not look", so `[]` is the documented way of asserting "nothing calls
+this" — a positive claim, made to a machine, contradicting the text half on the
+same index. Shipped in 1.1.9; patchnote written.
+
+**Fixed as a class, not an instance.** Both renderers now take a single
+`Attribution { name, declarations, decl_file }`, built only by `attribution_for`,
+so neither can be handed a raw query again and a future third consumer cannot
+repeat it. That is the §773 shape — deliver the invariant, not the point fix.
+
+**Mutations 7/7**, after one repair. **M3 (the JSON half ignores the pinned
+declaration) survived** — the text half had `the_callers_line_asks_about_a_declaration`
+with its `zqexecute` fixture and the JSON half had no equivalent, which is the
+same sibling gap in the TESTS as the one in the code. It now runs the same
+fixture with the same assertion, plus an anti-vacuity arm proving the name-keyed
+answer really does carry the phantom.
+
+Also added: `the_json_half_answers_what_the_text_half_answers`, a parity guard
+over one index, and M7 (`[]` instead of `null` on an unreadable graph) which the
+existing `json_callers_are_null_not_empty` guard kills.
+
+## §787 — le banc, et le classement qui m'envoyait sur une cible à 0,2 % (CLOSED)
+
+Première itération sous `docs/_loop-charter.md`. Pas de défaut produit : c'est
+l'itération d'outillage que la charte prévoit, et elle a corrigé un instrument
+avant qu'il ne coûte une itération entière.
+
+**Le banc existait à moitié.** `tokenade-bench` portait 4 binaires (~3 000
+lignes) et **aucun `lib.rs`** — et surtout, tous mesurent la BIBLIOTHÈQUE en
+process (`dispatch_named`). C'est pour ça qu'aucun n'avait jamais rencontré mes
+37 fautes d'instrument : elles sont toutes des propriétés du chemin **CLI de
+bout en bout**. Le manque était donc précis, pas général.
+
+`crates/tokenade-bench/src/lib.rs` est maintenant l'endroit unique où ce chemin
+se mesure, et il paie une fois les six pièges : sortie capturée en octets (aucun
+hook en chemin), état neuf par banc (`TOKENADE_CONFIG_DIR` / `TOKENADE_STASH_DIR`
+/ `TOKENADE_DEDUP_DB`), vraie extension obligatoire, profil déclaré avec
+`ms_comparable()` qui **refuse** un temps debug, `survived()` sur `code.is_some()`
+(un kill par signal n'est pas un succès), et `conclude()` qui **refuse de rendre
+« rien trouvé » sans canari**. 9 gardes.
+
+Deux corrections que l'écriture a imposées, toutes deux consignées dans le banc :
+
+- **L'exception d'isolement est nommée et gardée.** `gain`/`dashboard`/`health`
+  ont pour réponse l'état de l'opérateur ; les isoler rendrait un registre vide.
+  `reading_real_state()` existe pour ça et `run()` **refuse** toute autre
+  commande sur un banc non isolé.
+- **La construction est totale.** Elle ne résout plus le binaire en échouant :
+  sur un clone neuf `target/` est vide et toute la suite du workspace serait
+  tombée. L'absence surgit dans `run()`, avec le message qui dit quoi faire.
+
+**Le classement de valeur était faux, et sa PREMIÈRE utilisation l'a montré.**
+Il triait par octets encore livrés sans les rapporter au total, donc il m'a
+envoyé sur `sysinfo` : 59 307 usages — et 3 879 492 octets d'entrée, soit **65
+octets par appel**, 1,6 Mo de gain restant sur 833 Mo économisés (**0,2 %**).
+Corrigé, l'image s'inverse : **76 % de tout ce qui est encore livré vient de DEUX
+opérations**, `html-to-markdown` (40,9 %) et `auto-compact` (35,0 %), déjà à
+−95 % mais si volumineuses que leur résidu domine. Gagner un point sur la
+première rapporte plus que perfectionner `sysinfo` entièrement.
+
+Et **122 des 216 opérations du registre sont mes propres sondes** (concentrées
+sur un seul jour, moins de 50 appels) — écartées, et nommées dans le fichier.
+
+Le générateur vit désormais dans le dépôt
+(`cargo run -p tokenade-bench --bin value_ranking`), plus dans un script jetable.
+3 tests, dont celui qui fixe la règle : trier sur le taux seul sur-pondère le
+minuscule, sur le nombre d'usages seul sur-pondère le fréquent.
+
+**Prochaine cible, mesurée et non devinée : `html-to-markdown`** (21,5 Mo encore
+livrés, 40,9 % du gisement), puis `auto-compact` (18,5 Mo, 35,0 %).
+
+**Et le garde du dépôt m'a repris, comme au §777.**
+`every_process_launcher_either_guards_its_work_or_says_why_not` balaye plus large
+que `cli/src` et `core/src` — il a trouvé `bench/src/lib.rs` et posé sa question :
+« qui tue ça si tokenade est SIGKILLé ? ». Elle est juste : une mesure peut lancer
+une commande longue, et `Command::output()` ne rend aucune poignée sur l'enfant.
+Le banc **adopte donc le faucheur** (`spawn` + `guard_group_against_our_death` +
+`stand_down`, groupe créé seulement si un terminal ne le possède pas déjà), au
+lieu de s'inscrire dans `EXEMPT`. Deuxième fois que ce garde désigne un vrai
+manque plutôt qu'une formalité.
